WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:06.350
Netflix's post-apocalyptic thriller Bird Box
was its final hit of 2018, but now, the streamer

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:10.260
has been hit with serious backlash over some
footage featured in the flick.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:14.540
Bird Box featured plenty of terrifying scenes,
but the sequence that has sparked the most

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:19.270
discussion wasn't fictional it was footage
from a real-life tragedy, and Netflix has

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.060
refused to remove it from the film.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:27.320
It recently came to light that the film incorporated
actual footage from a 2013 fiery train crash

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:33.129
in the town of Lac-Mégantic in Quebec, Canada,
when an unattended 74-car freight train transporting

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:35.420
crude oil rolled downward and derailed.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.160
Multiple tank cars exploded and caught fire,
resulting in 47 deaths.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.149
Several other individuals were reported missing
at the time, and have since been presumed

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:44.640
dead.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.800
Half of Lac-Mégantic's downtown area was
destroyed, and the majority of the downtown

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:53.799
buildings that didn't collapse or catch fire
in the incident were destroyed due to contamination.

00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:58.329
The scene featuring footage of the real-life
disaster happens early on in Bird Box, and

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:02.420
while the filmmakers only used small bits
of the tragedy, Lac-Mégantic residents were

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.500
quick to notice that the clips were indeed
from the deadly accident.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:10.500
Lac-Megantic Mayor Julie Morin told the Canadian
Broadcasting Corporation that she and the

00:01:10.509 --> 00:01:13.140
townspeople hoped that Netflix would remove
the footage.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:13.740
She explained,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.299
"We find that it's really a lack of respect,
to use these images as fiction and entertainment.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.689
It's hard enough for our citizens to see these
images when they are used normally and respectfully

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:23.320
on the news.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.000
Just imagine, to have them used as fiction,
as if they were invented."

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.660
Morin also noticed that the sci-fi series
Travelers — also on Netflix — had utilized

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.680
footage from the Lac-Mégantic disaster.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.420
One of the producers of the show came forward
to apologize and explain that the images used

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.060
were sourced through the stock footage website
Pond5.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.720
She added that there was, quote, "no intention
to dishonor the tragic events of 2013."

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.979
In a statement made to the Canadian Broadcasting
Corporation, she said that her team is working

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.200
to replace the footage.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.460
It didn't take long for the people of Quebec
to stand up against Netflix for utilizing

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:57.049
the footage in Bird Box, either.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:01.689
Quebec Culture Minister Nathalie Roy penned
a letter to Netflix's chief executive officer

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:07.520
Reed Hastings, slamming the company for its,
quote, "lack of compassion, empathy, and solidarity."

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.540
Netflix's public policy director Corie Wright
has since responded to the written complaint,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.520
explaining that the video and images won't
be removed from Bird Box.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.760
According to Bloomberg, Wright said in a letter,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.960
"The use of stock footage is a widespread
and long-standing practice in the film and

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:23.960
television industry.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.200
As a result, stock images are commonly used
within content on Netflix and on other services.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.780
This widespread use prevents us from making
the changes you request on finished content.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.740
[…] Going forward, we (and the broader industry)
can do better."

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.560
This is now the second wave of controversy
to crash over Bird Box, as the film stirred

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.840
up storms of contention when people began
participating in a dangerous social media

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.040
stunt known as "The Bird Box Challenge," in
which fans would blindfold themselves and

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.220
attempt to drive cars, walk through traffic,
or move down steps without getting hurt.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.840
Hopefully Netflix can learn something from
this and, as the company said, do better moving

00:02:58.840 --> 00:02:59.340
forward.

